# npm rewrites lockfiles with the platform's native line endings. On Windows
# that means CRLF, while these files are stored LF, and core.autocrlf is false
# so git commits the bytes verbatim. The result is that a six-package change
# lands as a 15,000-line whole-file diff that no one can review.
#
# Forcing eol=lf makes the checkout LF regardless of platform, so a regenerated
# lockfile diffs against the blob line for line. All three lockfiles are already
# LF in HEAD, so this introduces no renormalization.
package-lock.json text eol=lf

# Shell scripts run in Linux containers (setup-tools.sh is invoked as a postinstall
# inside the backend image). A CRLF checkout produces `\r`-terminated shebangs and
# `command not found` at build time.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Dockerfiles are read by the daemon, not the host shell, but CRLF in a RUN line
# leaks into the executed command the same way.
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile text eol=lf

# TypeScript and JSON, for the same reason as the lockfile above: any tool that
# writes these files with native line endings — a codegen script, an editor on a
# fresh clone, an agent appending to a file — turns a 50-line change into a
# whole-file diff nobody can review.
#
# Unlike the lockfile, this is NOT free: 71 files were stored CRLF in the index
# when this landed (35 .tsx, 32 .ts, 4 .json), so the commit adding these rules
# also renormalizes them. Doing both at once is the point — adding the rules
# alone would leave those rewrites to surface one at a time inside unrelated
# feature PRs, which is the churn this is meant to stop.
*.ts text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
